{"batchcomplete":true,"query":{"pages":[{"pageid":80310887,"ns":0,"title":"Margaret Wooster Curti","revisions":[{"revid":1372296421,"parentid":1353903645,"minor":true,"timestamp":"2026-08-31T04:23:47Z","size":3897,"sha1":"f9e958bf9e2470f45efcd33701b864f5eaf8c20a","slots":{"main":{"contentmodel":"wikitext","contentformat":"text/x-wiki","content":"{{short description|American psychologist (1892-1961)}}\n{{Infobox person\n| name        = Margaret Wooster Curti\n| image       = Photo of Margaret Wooster Curti.jpg\n| image_size  = \n| caption     = \n| birth_name  = Margaret Wooster\n| birth_date  = {{birth date|1892|2|8|mf=y}}\n| birth_place = [[Silver Creek, Nebraska]]\n| death_date  = {{death date and age|1961|9|19|1892|2|8|mf=y}}\n| death_place = Madison, Wisconsin\n| death_cause = \n| other_names = \n| known_for   = \n| education   = [[University of Nebraska–Lincoln|University of Nebraska]], University of Chicago\n| employer    = \n| occupation  = psychologist, researcher\n}}\n'''Margaret Wooster Curti''' (February 8, 1892 – September 19, 1961) was an American child psychologist, researcher and author.\n\nCurti nee Wooster was born in [[Silver Creek, Nebraska]] on February 8, 1892.<ref name=\"Feminist Voices\">{{cite web |title=Margaret Wooster Curti |url=https://feministvoices.com/profiles/margaret-wooster-curti |website=Feminist Voices |access-date=26 June 2025 |language=en}}</ref> She attended the [[University of Nebraska–Lincoln|University of Nebraska]] where she studied inder [[Harry Kirke Wolfe]], and the [[University of Chicago]] where she studied under [[Harvey A. Carr]], earning her PhD in 1920.<ref name=\"ANB\">{{cite web |last1=Harris |first1=Benjamin |last2=Curti |first2=Merle |title=Curti, Margaret Wooster |url=https://doi.org/10.1093/anb/9780198606697.article.1400872 |website=American National Biography |access-date=26 June 2025 |language=en |doi=10.1093/anb/9780198606697.article.1400872}}</ref> In 1925 she married fellow academic [[Merle Curti]] with whom she had two children.\n\nShe taught at [[Smith College]] from 1922 through 1937, [[Teachers College, Columbia University]] from 1937 through 1942, and then briefly at [[University of Wisconsin–Madison]].<ref name=\"ANB\"/>\n\nIn 1930 [[Longman|Longmans, Green & Co.]] published her book ''Child psychology''.<ref name=\"HathiTrust\">{{cite web |last1=Curti |first1=Margaret Wooster |title=Child psychology |url=https://catalog.hathitrust.org/Record/005765389 |website=HathiTrust |publisher=Longmans, Green and co. |access-date=2025-06-28 |archive-date=2024-07-09 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240709141748/https://catalog.hathitrust.org/Record/005765389 |url-status=live }}</ref>\n\nCurti researched [[cultural bias]] in testing, disputing the idea that race or economic class effect a child's intelligence.<ref name=\"ANB\"/>\n\nCurti died in Madison, Wisconsin on September 19, 1961.<ref name=\"Smith College Special Collections\">{{cite web |title=Margaret Wooster Curti papers |url=https://findingaids.smith.edu/repositories/2/resources/1032 |website=Smith College Special Collections |access-date=26 June 2025 |archive-date=12 September 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240912213849/https://findingaids.smith.edu/repositories/2/resources/1032 |url-status=live }}</ref>\n\nHer papers are in the [[Sophia Smith Collection]] at Smith College.<ref name=\"Smith College Special Collections\"/>\n\n== Further reading ==\n* [https://digitalcommons.unl.edu/dissertations/AAIEP32772/ Curti, Margaret Wooster, \"Experimental study of bright and dull children\"] (1915).\n\n==References==\n{{Reflist}}\n\n{{Authority control}}\n\n{{DEFAULTSORT:Curti, Margaret Wooster}}\n[[Category:1892 births]]\n[[Category:1961 deaths]]\n[[Category:20th-century American psychologists]]\n[[Category:University of Nebraska–Lincoln alumni]]\n[[Category:University of Chicago alumni]]\n[[Category:19th-century people from Nebraska]]\n[[Category:20th-century people from Nebraska]]\n[[Categor